{"storyId":"AMSD-1919","agent":"openspec","notes":"Defect in confirmEmail validation on the checkout form: the validate function at src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx (around lines 300-306) does a raw `value !== email` comparison, which is case-sensitive. Fix should normalize both values (e.g. lowercase/trim) before comparing, and the same normalization should be applied consistently at submission time so the email sent downstream is not accidentally split by casing.","storyKind":"defect","acceptanceCriteria":["When a user enters the same email address in the Email and Confirm Email fields but with different letter casing, the Checkout form does not show a mismatch validation error.","When a user enters genuinely different email addresses (differing by more than casing) in the Email and Confirm Email fields, the Checkout form still shows the existing mismatch validation error.","When the Confirm Email field is left empty or the Email field is empty, existing required-field validation behavior is unchanged.","When the form is submitted with an email/confirm-email pair that differs only in casing, submission proceeds without being blocked by the confirm-email validation error."],"description":"On the Go-Checkout page, the Email and Confirm Email fields are compared case-sensitively, so entering the same address with different letter casing (e.g. Name@Example.com vs name@example.com) incorrectly triggers a mismatch error. Email comparison between these two fields should be case-insensitive so identical addresses differing only by case are accepted without error.","title":"Go-Checkout Page - Email & Confirm Email Case Sensitivity","verificationCriteriaDetail":[{"criterion":"Given a value typed in the Email field, when the same address is typed into Confirm Email with different letter casing, the rendered checkout form shows no validation error message for the Confirm Email field","observer":"tester","surface":"rendered Checkout page form, Confirm Email f